WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.010 --> 00:00:03.272
Thank you all so
much for joining us.

00:00:03.272 --> 00:00:05.480
I'm Susan Wilson, a producer
with the Center for Art,

00:00:05.480 --> 00:00:06.920
Science, and Technology.

00:00:06.920 --> 00:00:09.160
As everyone knows the
pandemic has thrown all of us

00:00:09.160 --> 00:00:11.380
into a sudden dependence
on virtual events.

00:00:11.380 --> 00:00:13.840
We're all working together
on navigating this new world

00:00:13.840 --> 00:00:16.390
and getting through all of the
challenges and opportunities

00:00:16.390 --> 00:00:17.380
that it provides.

00:00:17.380 --> 00:00:19.180
As we all continue to
iterate on the best

00:00:19.180 --> 00:00:20.620
practices for
online events, we've

00:00:20.620 --> 00:00:22.330
begun to transition
now from simply

00:00:22.330 --> 00:00:25.420
trying to recreate and replace
traditional events to really

00:00:25.420 --> 00:00:27.610
taking advantage
of a virtual format

00:00:27.610 --> 00:00:29.350
to combine the liveness
and collaboration

00:00:29.350 --> 00:00:31.600
that you can find in person
with the technological and

00:00:31.600 --> 00:00:34.460
vocationally boundless
benefits of virtuality.

00:00:34.460 --> 00:00:36.710
And I am so pleased to
introduce Tiandra Ray and Peter

00:00:36.710 --> 00:00:38.830
Godart, who will be
sharing their experiences

00:00:38.830 --> 00:00:42.070
and their own best practices for
virtual events with us today.

00:00:42.070 --> 00:00:45.040
Tiandra is a 2015 graduate
of MIT in course four.

00:00:45.040 --> 00:00:47.590
She's an architectural
designer, educator, and advocate

00:00:47.590 --> 00:00:49.150
for restorative justice.

00:00:49.150 --> 00:00:51.312
Peter graduated from
MIT in 2015 with degrees

00:00:51.312 --> 00:00:53.020
in mechanical and
electrical engineering,

00:00:53.020 --> 00:00:55.900
and is currently pursuing a
PhD in mechanical engineering,

00:00:55.900 --> 00:00:58.960
looking at ways to use aluminum
waste to power desalinization

00:00:58.960 --> 00:01:00.130
and produce energy.

00:01:00.130 --> 00:01:02.560
He's also a jazz
pianist and composer.

00:01:02.560 --> 00:01:04.540
Together they co-organized
Space for Action:

00:01:04.540 --> 00:01:07.578
Rebuilding a Sustainable
World, a 90 minute performance

00:01:07.578 --> 00:01:09.370
conversation in April
that brought together

00:01:09.370 --> 00:01:12.580
leading musicians, scientists,
politicians, and activists

00:01:12.580 --> 00:01:15.310
to reflect on how the pandemic
is reshaping our relationship

00:01:15.310 --> 00:01:16.540
with the earth.

00:01:16.540 --> 00:01:18.640
We're really excited to
have them with us today

00:01:18.640 --> 00:01:21.223
to share some of their insights
from creating Space for Action

00:01:21.223 --> 00:01:22.570
and other virtual events.

00:01:22.570 --> 00:01:25.300
And with that, I'll turn it
over to Peter and Tiandra.

00:01:25.300 --> 00:01:27.040
I am Tiandra Ray.

00:01:27.040 --> 00:01:28.090
That is Peter Godart.

00:01:28.090 --> 00:01:31.000
Thank you for the beautiful
introduction, Susan.

00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:33.690
We are two MIT
alumni and now Peter

00:01:33.690 --> 00:01:39.670
is back in the MIT student role,
who are currently spearheading

00:01:39.670 --> 00:01:41.530
this collective called
Space for Action,

00:01:41.530 --> 00:01:44.930
and it's a group of
interdisciplinary folks.

00:01:44.930 --> 00:01:48.370
So we have artists,
we have educators,

00:01:48.370 --> 00:01:52.460
we have musicians,
scientists, activists,

00:01:52.460 --> 00:01:54.850
people from the Boston
community and a lot

00:01:54.850 --> 00:01:56.560
of people from
the MIT community,

00:01:56.560 --> 00:02:00.460
who are coming together to
curate events and exhibits

00:02:00.460 --> 00:02:06.010
that help the public process
the large issue of our time,

00:02:06.010 --> 00:02:08.553
which is climate change,
and how that affects

00:02:08.553 --> 00:02:10.720
our relationships with each
other, our relationships

00:02:10.720 --> 00:02:12.700
with the earth,
how it intersects

00:02:12.700 --> 00:02:16.060
with various social
justice issues

00:02:16.060 --> 00:02:21.820
and disproportionately affects
people of color in our country,

00:02:21.820 --> 00:02:23.570
America specifically.

00:02:23.570 --> 00:02:26.710
So we've had a lot of
success with events

00:02:26.710 --> 00:02:28.210
in the virtual realm.

00:02:28.210 --> 00:02:33.490
We actually didn't set out
as a virtual organization.

00:02:33.490 --> 00:02:39.010
We had plans to actually hold
a large event and installation

00:02:39.010 --> 00:02:42.490
at MIT this past spring
for Earth Day and of course

00:02:42.490 --> 00:02:44.900
as the pandemic hit things
shifted a little bit.

00:02:44.900 --> 00:02:50.350
So we had a few virtual
concerts, conversations,

00:02:50.350 --> 00:02:54.250
talks all mixed together,
these virtual experiences

00:02:54.250 --> 00:02:56.920
over the course of the
past several months.

00:02:56.920 --> 00:03:03.400
And Peter is going to show us a
little bit from our performance

00:03:03.400 --> 00:03:03.980
event.

00:03:03.980 --> 00:03:06.200
And we had speakers
from all over.

00:03:06.200 --> 00:03:08.830
We had Reverend
Mariama White-Hammond,

00:03:08.830 --> 00:03:11.890
who is a local
minister and climate

00:03:11.890 --> 00:03:13.690
activist in the Boston area.

00:03:13.690 --> 00:03:16.840
We had representative
Ayanna Pressley there.

00:03:16.840 --> 00:03:21.520
We had Bill McKibben and
we had Esperanza Spalding,

00:03:21.520 --> 00:03:22.730
which was really exciting.

00:03:22.730 --> 00:03:27.160
So she did some music, some
speaking, and we also brought

00:03:27.160 --> 00:03:33.760
in some MIT alumni and some
Berklee professors and alumni

00:03:33.760 --> 00:03:36.640
to do some original
compositions.

00:03:36.640 --> 00:03:40.540
So Peter, do you want
to show us those clips?

00:03:40.540 --> 00:03:43.850
Yeah and before I do,
so to accomplish all

00:03:43.850 --> 00:03:46.190
that Tiandra said that
we're trying to accomplish

00:03:46.190 --> 00:03:48.465
as an organization,
Space for Action,

00:03:48.465 --> 00:03:50.840
the most important thing for
us are holding these events,

00:03:50.840 --> 00:03:54.260
these engaging events,
where it's a conversation

00:03:54.260 --> 00:03:56.157
and it's also educational.

00:03:56.157 --> 00:03:57.740
And so we spent a
lot of time thinking

00:03:57.740 --> 00:04:01.770
about how to accomplish
this in a virtual setting.

00:04:01.770 --> 00:04:03.160
And so what I'm
about to show you

00:04:03.160 --> 00:04:07.400
are some clips from
our most recent event.

00:04:07.400 --> 00:04:09.260
And so what I want
you to look out for

00:04:09.260 --> 00:04:12.380
is just the diversity
in terms of content

00:04:12.380 --> 00:04:15.590
and in terms of communities
that we're engaging with,

00:04:15.590 --> 00:04:18.829
as well as how the
talks and performances

00:04:18.829 --> 00:04:23.780
flow and transition from
one end to the other.

00:04:23.780 --> 00:04:27.780
And note that there was a
breakout session in here

00:04:27.780 --> 00:04:30.800
that I will not be showing.

00:04:30.800 --> 00:04:35.690
But aside from that just take
it all in and take some notes.

00:04:35.690 --> 00:04:37.730
We'll discuss this
after I show you.

00:05:02.975 --> 00:05:13.065
(SINGING) Come together
all you parts of me.

00:05:16.530 --> 00:05:26.787
Scattered and carried by wind
and wave to distant lands.

00:05:30.920 --> 00:05:41.880
Let us remember that wholeness
is our natural state.

00:05:51.140 --> 00:05:55.690
We will now hear from Edmar
Colon and Tutti Druyan.

00:05:55.690 --> 00:05:58.430
Edmar is a saxophonist,
pianist, and composer

00:05:58.430 --> 00:06:00.380
from Puerto Rico,
who is currently

00:06:00.380 --> 00:06:02.590
on the faculty of the
Berklee College of Music.

00:06:02.590 --> 00:06:06.440
Tutti is a singer, songwriter,
actor, and voiceover artist,

00:06:06.440 --> 00:06:08.290
and they have both
performed worldwide.

00:06:08.290 --> 00:06:12.350
Today, they will be performing
Somewhere over the Rainbow.

00:06:12.350 --> 00:06:14.870
So it is my pleasure
to introduce my dear

00:06:14.870 --> 00:06:16.370
friends, Tutti and Edmar.

00:07:00.106 --> 00:07:09.549
(SINGING) Somewhere
over the rainbow.

00:07:17.080 --> 00:07:21.400
Good evening, I say to you,
recognizing that for some of us

00:07:21.400 --> 00:07:24.070
this actually is a
statement of faith.

00:07:24.070 --> 00:07:29.260
To say that it is a good
evening means to imagine and let

00:07:29.260 --> 00:07:32.260
go of some of the
anxiety that we feel.

00:07:37.460 --> 00:07:40.610
We have an opportunity
here, an obligation,

00:07:40.610 --> 00:07:42.230
to build a more
equitable future.

00:07:42.230 --> 00:07:45.170
And I'm so grateful
for all of you

00:07:45.170 --> 00:07:47.900
frontliners, you
community builders,

00:07:47.900 --> 00:07:50.990
to be your sister in solidarity
and actualizing that vision.

00:07:50.990 --> 00:07:54.110
And for those that don't get
it I have great confidence

00:07:54.110 --> 00:07:58.400
in your powers of persuasion
and influence and storytelling

00:07:58.400 --> 00:07:59.760
to bring others along.

00:07:59.760 --> 00:08:00.260
Thank you.

00:08:34.789 --> 00:08:37.630
Our next special guest is
a true force of nature,

00:08:37.630 --> 00:08:39.409
thus since the
inception of her career

00:08:39.409 --> 00:08:42.710
has been propelling her field
forward with mighty speed

00:08:42.710 --> 00:08:45.980
and depth, while inspiring
many to explore their highest

00:08:45.980 --> 00:08:49.130
potential as artists
and human beings.

00:08:49.130 --> 00:08:52.190
Please help me virtually welcome
Grammy award-winning artists

00:08:52.190 --> 00:08:55.940
and big advocate for climate
and natural environment,

00:08:55.940 --> 00:08:59.960
and paraphrasing from
Reverend Mariama's words,

00:08:59.960 --> 00:09:01.550
I get to call her
my dear friend,

00:09:01.550 --> 00:09:03.245
my sister, Esperanza Spalding.

00:09:10.775 --> 00:09:12.790
(SINGING) I'm out,
the house of worship.

00:09:12.790 --> 00:09:14.420
Rife with pride,
rife with doctrine.

00:09:14.420 --> 00:09:16.495
Rife with sin, now
my laptop or device

00:09:16.495 --> 00:09:18.220
is where I shop for wisdom.

00:09:18.220 --> 00:09:21.950
At my very fingertips so
much more than some holy man

00:09:21.950 --> 00:09:23.200
could ever hope to comprehend.

00:09:23.200 --> 00:09:25.430
So maybe now the
holy spirit is simply

00:09:25.430 --> 00:09:28.490
a mighty network anchored
in accumulated knowledge.

00:09:28.490 --> 00:09:29.860
Now encompassing the globe.

00:09:33.982 --> 00:09:36.110
(SINGING) Heal our heart.

00:09:36.110 --> 00:09:39.605
Be like the drum, loud and sure.

00:09:42.540 --> 00:09:47.930
Feel the life rush through
our veins, cool and pure.

00:09:50.730 --> 00:09:57.770
Now the oneness of our
spirit shall endure.

00:09:57.770 --> 00:10:01.425
Say oh yeah, oh yeah, come.

00:10:08.830 --> 00:10:09.330
Oh, man.

00:10:09.330 --> 00:10:11.760
It's really hard to stop
a lot of those clips.

00:10:11.760 --> 00:10:14.458
But that was just a
couple of snippets.

00:10:14.458 --> 00:10:16.500
If you would like to see
the whole thing in full,

00:10:16.500 --> 00:10:17.550
it's up on YouTube.

00:10:17.550 --> 00:10:19.943
I'm happy to share
that with you.

00:10:19.943 --> 00:10:21.360
You'll get our
contact information

00:10:21.360 --> 00:10:23.070
at the end of this presentation.

00:10:23.070 --> 00:10:25.800
But to make this
engaging we're hoping

00:10:25.800 --> 00:10:28.990
that for this next
part, if you're able,

00:10:28.990 --> 00:10:30.990
we're going to send you
all into breakout rooms.

00:10:30.990 --> 00:10:32.370
We hope that, if
you're able, you

00:10:32.370 --> 00:10:36.540
can share your video
and unmute yourselves

00:10:36.540 --> 00:10:40.590
and in groups of
about five or so,

00:10:40.590 --> 00:10:43.530
we would like you to answer
these two questions, talk

00:10:43.530 --> 00:10:46.920
about these two questions
for about 10 minutes or so.

00:10:46.920 --> 00:10:49.890
And then we'll reconvene
and discuss and try

00:10:49.890 --> 00:10:52.390
to coalesce your responses.

00:10:52.390 --> 00:10:52.890
All right.

00:10:52.890 --> 00:10:54.240
Welcome back everybody.

00:10:54.240 --> 00:11:01.720
So now we are going to
talk about these questions

00:11:01.720 --> 00:11:03.160
as a group.

00:11:03.160 --> 00:11:04.770
We'll call on a
couple representatives

00:11:04.770 --> 00:11:09.903
from each breakout room
to discuss some insights

00:11:09.903 --> 00:11:10.820
that you came up with.

00:11:10.820 --> 00:11:16.930
And we're going to use
this jam board to document

00:11:16.930 --> 00:11:18.220
some of these responses.

00:11:21.260 --> 00:11:24.280
So I know, Stacy, you
were taking some notes.

00:11:24.280 --> 00:11:27.320
I'm going to cold call on
you to maybe kick us off.

00:11:27.320 --> 00:11:27.820
Yeah.

00:11:27.820 --> 00:11:28.320
Sure.

00:11:28.320 --> 00:11:29.500
I did take some notes.

00:11:29.500 --> 00:11:32.110
So we talked about
first what makes

00:11:32.110 --> 00:11:35.350
an in-person event so special.

00:11:35.350 --> 00:11:37.180
We talked about how
you can see people more

00:11:37.180 --> 00:11:41.440
and you get more nonverbal cues.

00:11:41.440 --> 00:11:43.300
I did mention that
at MIT there's

00:11:43.300 --> 00:11:47.380
usually snacks and
beverages, which is a plus.

00:11:47.380 --> 00:11:49.270
There's a networking
aspect and it feels

00:11:49.270 --> 00:11:51.430
a little bit more collegial.

00:11:51.430 --> 00:11:53.830
Multiple people can be
talking or discussing

00:11:53.830 --> 00:11:55.820
something at the same time.

00:11:55.820 --> 00:11:57.790
And you can move around
the physical space

00:11:57.790 --> 00:11:59.770
instead of sitting in
front of a screen, which

00:11:59.770 --> 00:12:03.370
I think helps a little
bit with event fatigue.

00:12:03.370 --> 00:12:06.310
And then moving into
successful versus

00:12:06.310 --> 00:12:07.840
unsuccessful online events.

00:12:07.840 --> 00:12:12.130
We talked a lot about practicing
and rehearsing and making sure

00:12:12.130 --> 00:12:15.160
that your presenters
understand the technology,

00:12:15.160 --> 00:12:19.090
having a really professional
run sheet and tech backups

00:12:19.090 --> 00:12:21.910
and the more freeform things
are, the more stressful

00:12:21.910 --> 00:12:23.470
they can become.

00:12:23.470 --> 00:12:25.510
We also talked about how
it's really important

00:12:25.510 --> 00:12:27.940
to understand the attention
span of most people.

00:12:27.940 --> 00:12:31.270
You can't really have
an hour lecture in zoom.

00:12:31.270 --> 00:12:33.640
And with that we
wanted to provide

00:12:33.640 --> 00:12:35.410
an opportunity for
people to interact

00:12:35.410 --> 00:12:39.190
and we noted that breakout rooms
are really effective for that.

00:12:39.190 --> 00:12:42.130
We've had some success across
multiple different departments

00:12:42.130 --> 00:12:44.080
with different types
of icebreaker or games

00:12:44.080 --> 00:12:45.538
where people get
to know each other

00:12:45.538 --> 00:12:47.560
and interact a little bit.

00:12:47.560 --> 00:12:50.135
And then we talked
about how if you're

00:12:50.135 --> 00:12:52.510
kind of planning your one-off
show, if you have something

00:12:52.510 --> 00:12:54.723
a little surprising and
something kind of engaging

00:12:54.723 --> 00:12:56.140
in an other
discussion points, you

00:12:56.140 --> 00:12:59.430
can have actually a
really engaging meeting.

00:12:59.430 --> 00:13:01.410
So those were our points.

00:13:05.830 --> 00:13:06.330
Great.

00:13:06.330 --> 00:13:08.220
Marion or Shannon
Rose, anything to add?

00:13:11.190 --> 00:13:11.690
No.

00:13:11.690 --> 00:13:14.540
That was a pretty
comprehensive representation.

00:13:14.540 --> 00:13:15.890
Thanks Stacy.

00:13:15.890 --> 00:13:17.300
Thank you.

00:13:17.300 --> 00:13:17.800
Cool.

00:13:17.800 --> 00:13:20.390
So anyone from the
other room, would you

00:13:20.390 --> 00:13:24.470
like to share some insights
as to these two questions?

00:13:24.470 --> 00:13:31.070
I was nominated, so I will share
our insight at room number two.

00:13:31.070 --> 00:13:35.390
We had a lot of overlap in
what was discussed just now.

00:13:35.390 --> 00:13:37.940
I would say successful
in-person events,

00:13:37.940 --> 00:13:40.310
some things that we
discussed that have not yet

00:13:40.310 --> 00:13:44.450
been mentioned are the
cultivation of atmosphere

00:13:44.450 --> 00:13:47.000
in in-person events
and what an impact that

00:13:47.000 --> 00:13:50.370
makes on your experience
as an audience.

00:13:50.370 --> 00:13:55.100
We also talked a little bit
about other people, generally.

00:13:55.100 --> 00:13:57.050
But I would classify,
rather than networking,

00:13:57.050 --> 00:13:59.240
what we talked about
more as intimacy,

00:13:59.240 --> 00:14:02.060
like noticing other things
that other people are saying,

00:14:02.060 --> 00:14:04.700
reacting to stimulus
with other people,

00:14:04.700 --> 00:14:08.360
having conversations that are
spontaneous and spontaneity is

00:14:08.360 --> 00:14:12.300
like another successful
in-person event trademark,

00:14:12.300 --> 00:14:12.800
I think.

00:14:12.800 --> 00:14:16.100
The ability to be spontaneous
in the conversation

00:14:16.100 --> 00:14:17.790
that you're having.

00:14:17.790 --> 00:14:20.450
In direct opposition
to that, it was

00:14:20.450 --> 00:14:25.200
Heidi that mentioned how
successful virtual events need

00:14:25.200 --> 00:14:28.842
to have some sort of
a moderator or agenda.

00:14:28.842 --> 00:14:31.050
If everyone's talking at
once and it's sort of a free

00:14:31.050 --> 00:14:33.270
for all, it's really
difficult to have a meaningful

00:14:33.270 --> 00:14:35.640
conversation because you
literally cannot hear each

00:14:35.640 --> 00:14:39.450
other speak because the audio
won't take multiple inputs

00:14:39.450 --> 00:14:41.100
at the same time.

00:14:41.100 --> 00:14:42.720
I personally just
find it really hard

00:14:42.720 --> 00:14:44.850
to have conversations
on Zoom for that reason

00:14:44.850 --> 00:14:47.730
because so much of
my conversation style

00:14:47.730 --> 00:14:52.230
is like spitballing.

00:14:52.230 --> 00:14:56.150
So I find it really hard to
talk to people on Zoom the way

00:14:56.150 --> 00:14:59.527
that I would normally speak.

00:14:59.527 --> 00:15:01.360
Another element of
successful virtual events

00:15:01.360 --> 00:15:06.610
that we talked about
was interactivity,

00:15:06.610 --> 00:15:08.260
which Stacey mentioned.

00:15:08.260 --> 00:15:11.800
We were talking about how
having an interactive element

00:15:11.800 --> 00:15:15.310
makes it easier to pay
attention and feel engaged

00:15:15.310 --> 00:15:18.100
in the online meeting
that you're in,

00:15:18.100 --> 00:15:21.890
which can be really nice.

00:15:21.890 --> 00:15:24.370
I think that that's
pretty much everything

00:15:24.370 --> 00:15:27.893
that diverged from
what Stacy mentioned.

00:15:27.893 --> 00:15:28.810
Am I missing anything?

00:15:28.810 --> 00:15:33.990
Maybe Heidi or Lydia could
chime in, or Catherine.

00:15:33.990 --> 00:15:37.000
The spontaneity part,
I would just add,

00:15:37.000 --> 00:15:39.090
we talked about
running into people,

00:15:39.090 --> 00:15:41.400
which is harder to do
when you're on Zoom,

00:15:41.400 --> 00:15:44.250
just the spontaneity of being
out and about at like a Boston

00:15:44.250 --> 00:15:46.860
theatre performance and
seeing someone you know well

00:15:46.860 --> 00:15:48.190
or someone you don't know well.

00:15:48.190 --> 00:15:54.120
But I guess it happens sometimes
on Zoom but not as much.

00:15:54.120 --> 00:15:56.030
Totally.

00:15:56.030 --> 00:15:57.650
Well, I'm super impressed.

00:15:57.650 --> 00:16:01.370
You all are experts on
virtual events, it seems.

00:16:01.370 --> 00:16:03.650
At least you've been to
enough good and bad ones

00:16:03.650 --> 00:16:06.440
to know the difference.

00:16:06.440 --> 00:16:09.250
So I'm just going to try to--

00:16:09.250 --> 00:16:11.560
you've all touched on
all of these things

00:16:11.560 --> 00:16:15.560
that I'm about to tell
you, but our philosophy

00:16:15.560 --> 00:16:20.030
with our event, which you
know we got good feedback on.

00:16:20.030 --> 00:16:23.050
And I tried to distill a lot of
the feedback that we'd received

00:16:23.050 --> 00:16:25.160
and it was very much
in line with what

00:16:25.160 --> 00:16:26.570
we were all discussing here.

00:16:26.570 --> 00:16:29.900
But to kind of distill those
into three guiding principles.

00:16:29.900 --> 00:16:32.150
We would put them into
these three categories.

00:16:32.150 --> 00:16:35.060
Brevity, keep things
short, keep things moving.

00:16:35.060 --> 00:16:37.045
As you all mentioned,
attention spans

00:16:37.045 --> 00:16:39.170
are low especially when
you're staring at a screen.

00:16:39.170 --> 00:16:42.120
You're not up and about
and moving around.

00:16:42.120 --> 00:16:44.870
And so it's best to avoid long
lectures wherever possible

00:16:44.870 --> 00:16:47.730
with only a couple of speakers.

00:16:47.730 --> 00:16:50.210
So if you are going
to be introducing

00:16:50.210 --> 00:16:52.470
a lot of spoken
or verbal content,

00:16:52.470 --> 00:16:54.980
it's good to either switch
back and forth between people

00:16:54.980 --> 00:16:57.840
or just have as many
speakers as possible.

00:16:57.840 --> 00:17:00.530
The second guiding principle
would be diversity.

00:17:00.530 --> 00:17:03.290
So it's super important
to include varied content

00:17:03.290 --> 00:17:05.780
and also varied representation
from different groups.

00:17:11.013 --> 00:17:12.680
The representation
from different groups

00:17:12.680 --> 00:17:16.628
is key because
different speakers

00:17:16.628 --> 00:17:18.920
are going to be engaging to
different groups of people.

00:17:18.920 --> 00:17:21.069
And so you want to have
content for everyone

00:17:21.069 --> 00:17:23.569
that you expect to be on the
call and maybe even some people

00:17:23.569 --> 00:17:25.010
you don't expect.

00:17:25.010 --> 00:17:27.119
And then the third
point is continuity.

00:17:27.119 --> 00:17:29.810
So no matter what you're
doing, try to tell a story,

00:17:29.810 --> 00:17:33.590
maintain a clear theme, and
transitions in particular

00:17:33.590 --> 00:17:34.250
are key.

00:17:38.500 --> 00:17:40.332
And a couple of Zoom tips.

00:17:40.332 --> 00:17:42.040
And again, these are
all basically things

00:17:42.040 --> 00:17:43.480
that people mentioned.

00:17:43.480 --> 00:17:46.060
We found that it was really
good to avoid the webinar

00:17:46.060 --> 00:17:50.350
style of events, where there's
this extra layer of anonymity,

00:17:50.350 --> 00:17:53.140
where you can't even see the
people who are on the call.

00:17:53.140 --> 00:17:56.182
It's nice to have
names and faces,

00:17:56.182 --> 00:17:57.640
and especially as
a performer, it's

00:17:57.640 --> 00:17:59.950
really nice to look
out to your Zoom screen

00:17:59.950 --> 00:18:01.810
and kind of imagine an audience.

00:18:01.810 --> 00:18:04.330
Seeing all the
squares is actually

00:18:04.330 --> 00:18:06.790
very inspiring to a performer.

00:18:06.790 --> 00:18:08.665
Have a script and
practice the transitions,

00:18:08.665 --> 00:18:09.790
including sharing a screen.

00:18:09.790 --> 00:18:11.860
We've all been on
these Zoom meetings

00:18:11.860 --> 00:18:13.943
where people don't know
how to share their screen,

00:18:13.943 --> 00:18:15.670
or unmute themselves, et cetera.

00:18:15.670 --> 00:18:21.130
Mistakes obviously happen, but
just try to be prepared there.

00:18:21.130 --> 00:18:23.680
Frequent use of breakout rooms
and just encouraging people

00:18:23.680 --> 00:18:26.230
to show video and keeping
them engaged at various points

00:18:26.230 --> 00:18:29.290
throughout, obviously
is a great idea.

00:18:29.290 --> 00:18:31.480
For musicians, it's
extremely important

00:18:31.480 --> 00:18:33.260
to set up the audio correctly.

00:18:33.260 --> 00:18:35.410
So there are all these
advanced audio settings

00:18:35.410 --> 00:18:38.650
that turn off the
Zoom algorithms,

00:18:38.650 --> 00:18:42.730
which are optimized for speech,
and in particular this option

00:18:42.730 --> 00:18:44.860
called turn on original
sound, which just takes

00:18:44.860 --> 00:18:46.568
your raw input from
the microphone, which

00:18:46.568 --> 00:18:49.690
is great for live instruments.

00:18:49.690 --> 00:18:51.730
As someone mentioned
in our group, at least,

00:18:51.730 --> 00:18:54.850
it's important to have a
moderator in any large group

00:18:54.850 --> 00:18:56.140
discussions.

00:18:56.140 --> 00:18:57.820
People can't be
trusted to sort of self

00:18:57.820 --> 00:19:00.960
organize when there's
20 or so people.

00:19:00.960 --> 00:19:02.710
People are going to
talk over one another,

00:19:02.710 --> 00:19:04.630
and then the best
case scenario is

00:19:04.630 --> 00:19:06.110
that everyone is just silent.

00:19:06.110 --> 00:19:08.110
So it's good to break it
up into smaller groups,

00:19:08.110 --> 00:19:12.370
or if it is a larger group,
obviously, have a moderator.

00:19:12.370 --> 00:19:15.160
Using a team and spreading
out responsibilities

00:19:15.160 --> 00:19:17.230
is extremely helpful.

00:19:17.230 --> 00:19:19.390
You can have someone
managing break out rooms,

00:19:19.390 --> 00:19:23.650
someone speaking,
and someone sitting

00:19:23.650 --> 00:19:29.280
idle ready to unmute the person
who forgot to mute themselves

00:19:29.280 --> 00:19:34.410
and is yelling at their dog,
which literally happened to us.

00:19:34.410 --> 00:19:38.850
And in general just be positive
and embrace the format.

00:19:38.850 --> 00:19:40.920
Obviously, it's
not a live event,

00:19:40.920 --> 00:19:43.230
and I think there
are some benefits

00:19:43.230 --> 00:19:47.910
to doing virtual events so just
try to embrace those benefits.

00:19:47.910 --> 00:19:50.660
And by the way, I will be
sharing these slides so

00:19:50.660 --> 00:19:54.550
don't feel like you have to
take notes on all of this.

00:19:54.550 --> 00:19:58.310
I'd be happy to share
this afterwards.

00:19:58.310 --> 00:20:01.278
So, Tiandra, do you want to
talk about some things that are

00:20:01.278 --> 00:20:02.570
on Zoom, which are pretty cool?

00:20:02.570 --> 00:20:03.560
Yes.

00:20:03.560 --> 00:20:07.520
So we all know and love
Zoom at this point,

00:20:07.520 --> 00:20:10.220
or well complicated
relationship with Zoom maybe.

00:20:10.220 --> 00:20:13.965
But we all have interacted
with this format

00:20:13.965 --> 00:20:16.340
and are interacting with it
now, but there are definitely

00:20:16.340 --> 00:20:19.700
ways to enhance
the Zoom experience

00:20:19.700 --> 00:20:24.350
by adding in elements that
go beyond just the video.

00:20:24.350 --> 00:20:27.410
And just to give
you a little preview

00:20:27.410 --> 00:20:31.040
of what Peter and I have been
cooking up with the SFA team.

00:20:31.040 --> 00:20:34.280
We're thinking about physical
installations that are

00:20:34.280 --> 00:20:40.460
interactive via a stream,
more interactive from afar

00:20:40.460 --> 00:20:42.950
and thinking about how we
organize people in outdoor

00:20:42.950 --> 00:20:47.180
spaces, but streaming it so that
it is accessible to people who

00:20:47.180 --> 00:20:49.680
cannot access the
outdoor spaces and again,

00:20:49.680 --> 00:20:55.310
different ways of interactivity
virtually that is not just us

00:20:55.310 --> 00:20:58.220
having a conversation and
interacting with physical built

00:20:58.220 --> 00:21:00.660
space, which is
really interesting.

00:21:00.660 --> 00:21:03.440
But I want to point out
a couple of other tools

00:21:03.440 --> 00:21:06.710
that we've interacted with
in the last few months.

00:21:06.710 --> 00:21:10.100
So High Fidelity is
an interesting tool.

00:21:10.100 --> 00:21:15.290
I think it was Heidi that
mentioned in our group enjoying

00:21:15.290 --> 00:21:17.540
just bumping into people
and being able to hear

00:21:17.540 --> 00:21:19.200
a conversation from afar.

00:21:19.200 --> 00:21:25.190
So what High Fidelity does is
it places everybody on a map

00:21:25.190 --> 00:21:30.380
that you design, like a map of
the space, and it's a 2D map.

00:21:30.380 --> 00:21:34.040
And Peter, you could click
on that link I think,

00:21:34.040 --> 00:21:36.470
not the picture, the caption.

00:21:36.470 --> 00:21:38.520
It takes you to that page.

00:21:38.520 --> 00:21:42.770
So you're actually just a
floating head on this map

00:21:42.770 --> 00:21:45.888
and you actually hear
in your headphones

00:21:45.888 --> 00:21:47.930
the people talking around
you, depending on where

00:21:47.930 --> 00:21:49.310
your icon is on the map.

00:21:49.310 --> 00:21:54.470
So Peter and I attended this
birthday party festival thing,

00:21:54.470 --> 00:21:56.870
where they use this and
it was pretty interesting.

00:21:56.870 --> 00:21:58.670
There are definitely
some kinks to work out

00:21:58.670 --> 00:22:03.587
in terms of not being awkward,
but when you're with friends,

00:22:03.587 --> 00:22:05.420
I think it especially
is helpful when you're

00:22:05.420 --> 00:22:07.045
with a group of people
that you already

00:22:07.045 --> 00:22:08.600
have some familiarity with.

00:22:08.600 --> 00:22:11.560
But the points on the map can be
linked to do different things.

00:22:11.560 --> 00:22:15.050
So we talked about, whether you
could link a portion of the map

00:22:15.050 --> 00:22:16.110
to a Zoom meeting.

00:22:16.110 --> 00:22:19.062
So you go to a mini
breakout room and just

00:22:19.062 --> 00:22:20.270
interesting things like that.

00:22:20.270 --> 00:22:24.710
They mapped it to a
series of Google photos

00:22:24.710 --> 00:22:28.460
from different past events
to make people feel nostalgic

00:22:28.460 --> 00:22:30.560
and hid a scavenger
hunt inside of there.

00:22:30.560 --> 00:22:34.262
So there are lots of different
options for interaction there.

00:22:34.262 --> 00:22:35.720
And then there are
tools like MURAL

00:22:35.720 --> 00:22:38.220
boards or the Jamboard
that you just saw,

00:22:38.220 --> 00:22:41.330
which are great for
collaborative brainstorming.

00:22:41.330 --> 00:22:44.270
And of course
there's the whatever

00:22:44.270 --> 00:22:50.090
you all decide to come up with
in your innovative, creative

00:22:50.090 --> 00:22:51.320
capacities.

00:22:51.320 --> 00:22:54.680
So we want to have a quick
