00:28:42.690 --> 00:28:45.900
was prerecorded versus live?

00:28:45.900 --> 00:28:48.256
It was 100% live.

00:28:48.256 --> 00:28:48.756
100% live?

00:28:48.756 --> 00:28:49.256
Amazing.

00:28:49.256 --> 00:28:50.130
Digital recording.

00:28:50.130 --> 00:28:50.775
Yeah.

00:28:50.775 --> 00:28:51.400
That's awesome.

00:28:51.400 --> 00:28:54.880
Risky, especially that
first piece, which we

00:28:54.880 --> 00:28:59.880
kind of slipped in last
minute to have the drum

00:28:59.880 --> 00:29:02.015
and beats coming from
one place and Peter

00:29:02.015 --> 00:29:03.640
turning on the piano
from another place

00:29:03.640 --> 00:29:06.110
and Ricky singing so.

00:29:06.110 --> 00:29:10.130
I'd encourage from that
for everyone to experiment,

00:29:10.130 --> 00:29:12.980
push the boundaries.

00:29:12.980 --> 00:29:16.790
You don't have to be
afraid of the lag.

00:29:16.790 --> 00:29:18.842
As musicians like
you can make it--

00:29:18.842 --> 00:29:20.300
there are definitely
certain things

00:29:20.300 --> 00:29:27.150
you can't do but get creative
and innovative with it.

00:29:27.150 --> 00:29:29.730
And I think Marion you were
talking about the lecture

00:29:29.730 --> 00:29:32.730
series and creating something
that's more engaging.

00:29:32.730 --> 00:29:36.300
Well, maybe that means that you
reimagine the lecture series

00:29:36.300 --> 00:29:37.740
and it's no longer
just a lecture.

00:29:37.740 --> 00:29:40.410
But it's more of an
interactive conversation

00:29:40.410 --> 00:29:44.210
and it's not just the person
speaking and then Q&amp;A.

00:29:44.210 --> 00:29:48.330
But maybe it's something
that reads differently.

00:29:48.330 --> 00:29:50.250
If people are open
to actually pushing

00:29:50.250 --> 00:29:53.390
that boundary at this point.

00:29:53.390 --> 00:29:54.365
That is the question.

00:29:56.797 --> 00:29:58.130
And I will say we had a lot of--

00:29:58.130 --> 00:29:59.780
But a great idea.

00:29:59.780 --> 00:30:01.430
--We had people
like Bill McKibben,

00:30:01.430 --> 00:30:03.210
Esperanza Spalding, Rep.

00:30:03.210 --> 00:30:05.480
Ayanna Presley,
and people really

00:30:05.480 --> 00:30:07.863
felt like they were on a
Zoom call with these people

00:30:07.863 --> 00:30:09.030
because they literally were.

00:30:09.030 --> 00:30:10.820
They felt like that you
were in a very small room.

00:30:10.820 --> 00:30:13.550
It was very intimate with these
just like incredible people

00:30:13.550 --> 00:30:17.270
you would not have access
to in any other setting.

00:30:17.270 --> 00:30:22.040
And we could have definitely
asked for prerecorded input

00:30:22.040 --> 00:30:24.830
from each of these
artists and speakers,

00:30:24.830 --> 00:30:26.420
but we really didn't want that.

00:30:26.420 --> 00:30:29.480
We took the risk and
just went for full live.

00:30:29.480 --> 00:30:33.770
And yeah I'd encourage you
all to take that risk as well.

00:30:38.000 --> 00:30:39.750
And they interacted
with each other, which

00:30:39.750 --> 00:30:43.530
was really beautiful, too.

00:30:43.530 --> 00:30:48.530
In allowing that
mix, the panel model

00:30:48.530 --> 00:30:51.260
almost works more
in this context

00:30:51.260 --> 00:30:54.170
because it's not just one person
talking but you can jump off

00:30:54.170 --> 00:30:54.770
of each other.

00:30:57.500 --> 00:31:02.170
So before we wrap up here, I am
going to share this last slide

00:31:02.170 --> 00:31:03.460
with everybody.

00:31:03.460 --> 00:31:06.610
And I would encourage, you
can take a screenshot of this.

00:31:06.610 --> 00:31:10.250
And this is our information
for Space for Action.

00:31:10.250 --> 00:31:12.640
And if you're interested
in our organization

00:31:12.640 --> 00:31:16.390
and want to get involved,
this is the information

00:31:16.390 --> 00:31:17.840
that you would need to do so.

00:31:17.840 --> 00:31:21.910
So we're planning some
hopefully in-person events

00:31:21.910 --> 00:31:22.880
when this is all over.

00:31:22.880 --> 00:31:25.760
But definitely some more virtual
events until that happens.

00:31:25.760 --> 00:31:32.038
And we'd love to just engage
more people so hit us up.

00:31:32.038 --> 00:31:33.580
Peter and Tiandra,
thank you so much.

00:31:33.580 --> 00:31:35.070
This was such a
wonderful workshop.

00:31:35.070 --> 00:31:36.945
It was great to hear
more about this reaction

00:31:36.945 --> 00:31:39.070
and I think it was really
an exciting conversation.

00:31:39.070 --> 00:31:41.185
We can't wait to have more
online events and then

00:31:41.185 --> 00:31:41.810
in-person ones.

00:31:41.810 --> 00:31:43.730
We're very excited
about that, too.

00:31:43.730 --> 00:31:45.410
Everyone please stick around.

00:31:45.410 --> 00:31:47.060
Arts on the Radar continues.

00:31:47.060 --> 00:31:50.227
So the ACT gallery opening
will be starting at 5:00

00:31:50.227 --> 00:31:51.810
and we're thrilled
about that as well.

00:31:51.810 --> 00:31:53.670
We have an exciting night.

00:31:53.670 --> 00:31:57.310
And thanks to Susan, Lydia,
and Shannon Rose for helping

00:31:57.310 --> 00:31:59.180
put all this together.

00:31:59.180 --> 00:32:00.940
This is fun for us as well.

00:32:00.940 --> 00:32:02.700
Thank you.

